In this lesson, we'll look at combinations, which are selections where order doesn't matter. So if you're picking three people for a committee, choosing Alice, Bob, and Cara is the same as choosing Cara, Bob, and Alice — it's just one group. The formula counts how many ways you can choose r objects from n distinct ones. Think of it as the arrangement formula, but with an extra step that divides out the repeated orderings. Now, a couple of handy facts to remember. Choosing nothing gives one result, and choosing everything also gives one result — so the formula equals one in both those cases. There's also a neat symmetry: choosing r items to include is the same as choosing the rest to leave out, so the formula gives the same answer either way. As a rule of thumb, ask yourself whether rearranging the picked items really changes things. If it does, like electing a president, secretary, and treasurer, you're dealing with an arrangement. If it doesn't, like forming a committee, it's a combination. Now let's see it in action.